Looking at the diff, one line was removed and one added in the `parse()` switch:

- **`case 'secs':` removed** — this is a real regression. The regex still matches `secs` (the alternation `secs?` is unchanged), so `'2 secs'`, `'3secs'`, `'2 SECS'`, etc. still produce `match[2] = 'secs'`, but the switch no longer has a matching case and falls to `default: return undefined`. Previously these returned the correct millisecond value (e.g. `2000`). The README documents `secs` as a supported unit, and downstream callers (and other test suites) use it — the library's own suite just happens not to cover it, which is why it stays green.
- **`case "":` added** — this is unreachable dead code. The capture group `(milliseconds?|…|y)?` either does not participate (giving `match[2] === undefined`, which `(match[2] || 'ms')` turns into `'ms'`) or matches one of the alternatives, each of which consumes at least one character. It can never yield the empty string, so this case can never fire.

So `ms('2 secs')` goes from `2000` to `undefined` — an observable behaviour change.

